<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Bundles Scribobulate's own icons that have no
  equivalent on any standard icon theme (Adwaita, Breeze, or otherwise —
  researcher-verified before adding this file), so they render reliably
  regardless of the host's installed icon theme instead of risking a silent
  broken-image placeholder. (Whether every OTHER icon name this app assumes is
  present actually resolves on a given host's icon theme is checked by
  `tests/icon_resolution.rs`, which renders each name and reports the
  file it resolved to — a query like `has_icon` is not equivalent, see
  ScrAP-169.)

  Compiled at build time by build.rs (glib_build_tools::compile_resources)
  into a GResource blob embedded in the binary; registered at startup via
  gio::resources_register_include! and GtkIconTheme::add_resource_path so
  `Button::from_icon_name("copy-document-symbolic")` resolves exactly like a
  system-theme icon would, including automatic light/dark symbolic
  recoloring (only achievable via a real GResource + IconTheme resource
  path, not a raw embedded texture).
-->
<gresources>
  <gresource prefix="/com/extollit/scribobulate/icons">
    <file>scalable/actions/copy-document-symbolic.svg</file>
    <!--
      Outline-panel header buttons (Expand all / Collapse all). Unlike
      copy-document-symbolic these DO exist in some themes (e.g. breeze-dark),
      but NOT in Adwaita (verified against the installed Adwaita symbolic set),
      so bundling our own guarantees they render on every host theme instead of
      showing a broken-image placeholder on Adwaita (ANTI-PATTERNS.md #39). On a
      theme that has these names the active theme's version wins; our copy is the
      fallback. This is DELIBERATE: the standard names let a KDE/breeze desktop
      show its own idiomatic expand-all/collapse-all glyphs (chevron-style),
      matching the host theme — our bundled chevron art (which mirrors that look)
      only fills in on themes lacking the names (e.g. Adwaita/headless).
    -->
    <file>scalable/actions/expand-all-symbolic.svg</file>
    <file>scalable/actions/collapse-all-symbolic.svg</file>
    <!--
      adwaita-icon-theme REMOVED both of these legacy freedesktop names in
      48.alpha ("symbolic: remove emblems", issue #287), so any host on a recent
      Adwaita loses them — this is icon-theme drift, not a property of any one
      OS. Measured: Adwaita 41 and the GNOME 46 runtime still carry both; 48+
      does not. Breeze still has both, so a KDE desktop keeps showing its own
      art — as with expand/collapse above, the host theme wins and our copy is
      the fallback.

      emblem-photos-symbolic (the "show unsafe images" toggle) has no
      non-symbolic sibling left either, so without this it renders the literal
      broken-image placeholder. emblem-synchronizing-symbolic (File ▸
      Auto-Reload) is the subtler of the two: 50.rc reinstated a legacy 16x16
      RASTER emblem-synchronizing.png, and GTK's lookup strips the `-symbolic`
      suffix and finds it — so the button LOOKS fine while silently rendering a
      baked-colour PNG that does not follow the theme foreground on a dark
      variant (ScrAP-169's hazard, arrived at through the fallback chain rather
      than through a mis-chosen name). Bundling a real symbolic SVG under the
      exact requested name pre-empts that fallback, because the `-symbolic`
      candidate is tried before the stripped one.

      Independently confirmed on Windows, where the drift is total rather than
      partial: gvsbuild's Adwaita 50.0 symbolic set ships NO `emblems` category
      at all (only emblem-important/emblem-system under `legacy`). Auto-Reload
      there drew the legacy full-colour raster amid a row of thin line glyphs —
      worse than a placeholder, because it looks deliberate.
    -->
    <file>scalable/emblems/emblem-photos-symbolic.svg</file>
    <file>scalable/emblems/emblem-synchronizing-symbolic.svg</file>
    <!--
      The application icon. Sits at the freedesktop path an app icon belongs at,
      so this one file serves both consumers without a copy or an alias: the
      GResource below, and install.sh (which mirrors the same relative path into
      hicolor). Any OS packaging step that rasterizes an app icon reads it from
      here too.

      Without it here, the About dialog's .logo_icon_name() and the window's
      .icon_name() resolve only if install.sh has been run — so every
      uninstalled `cargo run` showed the broken-image placeholder where the app
      logo belongs. Note this masks itself on a developer box: once install.sh
      has run, the icon resolves from hicolor forever after, so the bug is
      invisible to anyone who has ever installed. It is a normal
      full-colour icon, NOT symbolic: it must keep its own palette rather than
      being recoloured to the theme foreground.

      On Windows the same gap cannot mask itself, because there is no
      `install.sh` step at all — the staged tree ships only gvsbuild's stock
      Adwaita + hicolor, so the lookup found nothing and the title bar, taskbar
      and Alt+Tab all fell back to GTK's generic default. Bundling here makes the
      name resolve on EVERY platform. This file is the source of truth for the
      app icon: install.sh installs this path, and
      packaging/windows/scribobulate.ico is rendered from it.
    -->
    <file>scalable/apps/com.extollit.scribobulate.svg</file>
  </gresource>
</gresources>
